### What is this question really testing?

The interviewer wants your technology-evaluation rigor. They want to see that you compare candidates against project-specific criteria rather than picking whatever is popular, and that you can defend the choice with data.

```java
package com.bank.interview;

public class TechEvaluation {
    enum Method { BY_HYPE, BY_HABIT, BY_CRITERIA }

    public static String evaluate(Method method) {
        return switch (method) {
            case BY_HYPE     -> "Weak: chose because it is trending";
            case BY_HABIT    -> "Weak: chose because I already know it";
            case BY_CRITERIA -> "Strong: chose based on weighted project-specific factors";
        };
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(evaluate(Method.BY_CRITERIA));
    }
}
```

```text
Strong: chose based on weighted project-specific factors
```

### What are the core evaluation factors?

The factors cluster into groups. Problem fit comes first: does the technology solve your specific problem well? For a banking ledger at National Bank of India, strong ACID transactions dominate. Performance comes next, measured against your real load. Then team familiarity, ecosystem maturity, security track record, and operational cost.

```java
package com.bank.interview;

import java.util.List;

public class EvalFactors {
    record Factor(String name, double weight, String rationale) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Factor> factors = List.of(
            new Factor("Problem fit",       0.30, "Must support ACID transactions for money"),
            new Factor("Performance",       0.20, "Must handle peak transfer load"),
            new Factor("Team familiarity",  0.20, "Team can operate it at 3am during incident"),
            new Factor("Ecosystem maturity",0.15, "Security track record, community support"),
            new Factor("Operational cost",  0.15, "Monitoring, upgrading, running in production")
        );
        factors.forEach(f -> System.out.printf("%-20s weight=%.2f  %s%n", f.name(), f.weight(), f.rationale()));
    }
}
```

```text
Problem fit          weight=0.30  Must support ACID transactions for money
Performance          weight=0.20  Must handle peak transfer load
Team familiarity     weight=0.20  Team can operate it at 3am during incident
Ecosystem maturity   weight=0.15  Security track record, community support
Operational cost     weight=0.15  Monitoring, upgrading, running in production
```

### How do you weight criteria differently per project?

A senior nuance is weighting by what this specific project needs, not treating all factors equally. For a trading system, latency dominates. For an internal tool, developer speed dominates. For a ledger, correctness dominates.

```java
package com.bank.interview;

import java.util.Map;

public class ContextualWeighting {
    public static Map<String, Double> weightsFor(String projectType) {
        return switch (projectType) {
            case "ledger"   -> Map.of("fit", 0.40, "performance", 0.20, "maturity", 0.20, "team", 0.10, "ops", 0.10);
            case "trading"  -> Map.of("fit", 0.20, "performance", 0.40, "maturity", 0.15, "team", 0.15, "ops", 0.10);
            case "internal" -> Map.of("fit", 0.20, "performance", 0.10, "maturity", 0.10, "team", 0.40, "ops", 0.20);
            default         -> Map.of("fit", 0.20, "performance", 0.20, "maturity", 0.20, "team", 0.20, "ops", 0.20);
        };
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Ledger weights: " + weightsFor("ledger"));
    }
}
```

### How does a proof-of-concept convert opinion into evidence?

Running a small spike with the same workload on both candidates gives you real numbers for fit and performance instead of guesses. For Rajesh's ledger service, you test both datastores with the same mix of debits and credits.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.logging.Logger;

public class ProofOfConcept {
    private static final Logger log = Logger.getLogger(ProofOfConcept.class.getName());

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Simulated spike results
        long relationalP99 = 12;  // ms
        long documentP99 = 45;    // ms
        boolean relationalAcid = true;
        boolean documentAcid = false;

        log.info(() -> "Relational: p99=" + relationalP99 + "ms, ACID=" + relationalAcid);
        log.info(() -> "Document:   p99=" + documentP99 + "ms, ACID=" + documentAcid);
        log.info("Winner for ledger: Relational (ACID + lower latency)");
    }
}
```

```text
Relational: p99=12ms, ACID=true
Document:   p99=45ms, ACID=false
Winner for ledger: Relational (ACID + lower latency)
```

### How do you present a comparison table?

| Factor | Relational DB | Document DB |
|---|---|---|
| Problem fit (ACID) | Strong : native transactions | Weak : no multi-doc ACID |
| Performance at peak | 12ms p99 | 45ms p99 |
| Team familiarity | High : team has 5 years | Low : new to everyone |
| Maturity | Decades of security track record | Newer, less audited |
| Operational cost | Moderate : well-understood | Higher : unfamiliar ops |

```java
package com.bank.interview;

public class ComparisonResult {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Relational: fit=9, perf=8, team=9, maturity=9, ops=7 -> score=8.5");
        System.out.println("Document:   fit=4, perf=6, team=3, maturity=6, ops=5 -> score=4.6");
        System.out.println("Decision: Relational for the ledger.");
    }
}
```

### How do you acknowledge the losing option fairly?

A fair evaluation credits the losing option's genuine strengths. This shows you were not rationalizing a pre-made choice.

```java
package com.bank.interview;

public class FairCredit {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Document DB genuine strength: flexible schema for nested data.");
        System.out.println("We used it elsewhere for a reporting feature where that flexibility was the right fit.");
        System.out.println("It was not right for the ledger, but it was right for reporting.");
    }
}
```

### Why does reversibility matter in technology choices?

When uncertainty is high, prefer a choice that is easier to change later. A cheap-to-reverse decision deserves less agonizing than a one-way door.

```java
package com.bank.interview;

public class Reversibility {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        boolean highUncertainty = true;
        String advice = highUncertainty
            ? "Prefer the easily reversible option; invest less in deliberation."
            : "Both options are well-understood; pick on merit.";
        System.out.println(advice);
    }
}
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

Expect: "Would you make the same choice today?" (show reflection), "What if the team already knew the other option?" (show you weight team familiarity), and "How did the choice hold up over time?" (close with a measurable result).

```java
package com.bank.interview;

public class TechChoiceFollowUps {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Q: Same choice today? A: Yes, the ACID requirement has not changed.");
        System.out.println("Q: Team knew Document? A: Would re-weight team factor but fit still dominates.");
        System.out.println("Q: How did it hold up? A: Zero data-integrity issues in 18 months.");
    }
}
```
